}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A \code{configure} and \code{configure.win} resolve
\code{system.file("lib", .Platform$r_arch, package = "zucrypt")}, falling back
to \code{system.file("lib", package = "zucrypt")}, and substitute the result
into \code{src/Makevars.in} as a \strong{single-quoted} \code{PKG_LIBS} entry -- a library
path containing a space, which is the norm on Windows, otherwise reaches
the linker as two arguments. Then include \verb{<zucrypt.h>} and call the
functions directly.

This shape owns the backend's lifetime: call \code{zuc_init()} before anything
else and \code{zuc_shutdown()} when finished. It is reference counted, so
nesting is safe.

Two consequences worth stating plainly. Your shared object contains its own
copy of the backend, kept private by hidden visibility. And upgrading
zucrypt does nothing for you until your package is reinstalled -- including
a security fix -- so report the version you actually linked, by calling
\code{zuc_get_info()} rather than reading a macro.
}

\section{What the interface promises}{

\itemize{
\item Every function returns a \code{zuc_status}. None raises an R error, allocates
an R object, or calls back into R. No status is negative, so \verb{if (st)}
means "not success".
\item An object the library allocated is destroyed only by a library function.
No buffer you pass is retained after the call returns.
\item \code{inst/include/zucrypt.h} compiles standalone as C99 against \verb{<stddef.h>}
and \verb{<stdint.h>}, and names no backend type. You never have to reproduce
this package's build configuration.
\item Fields are appended to the table, never removed or reordered. A layout
change to any other type in the header renames the registered callable
instead, so an old consumer fails at \code{R_GetCCallable()} rather than
reading a structure that has moved.
\item Main thread only, in this version.
}
}

\section{Stability}{

\code{ZUCRYPT_ABI_VERSION} is \code{1}, frozen at v0.1.0. Within a major version:
functions and table fields may be added; nothing is removed, reordered or
given a new meaning; enumerator values are permanent, so an algorithm
compiled out of a build keeps its number and reports itself unavailable;
and a \verb{ZUC_*_REQUIRED_SIZE} macro never grows, so a consumer built against
an older header keeps working without being rebuilt.

A layout change to a type that \code{struct_size} cannot see renames the
registered callable instead, so an old consumer fails at
\code{R_GetCCallable()} rather than reading a structure that has moved.

Not promised: the numeric value of a backend status behind
\code{ZUC_ERR_BACKEND}, the contents of an opaque handle, and thread safety
beyond the main thread.
}

